WHY THIS ONE IS FLIP-CRITICAL:
The JS production worker at least guards migrate() behind a per-isolate
promise (ensureMigrated). The Rust port has NO guard: routes.rs
handle_inner calls migrate(db) unconditionally on EVERY request
(routes.rs:314), and scheduled() does the same (routes.rs:1891).
migrate() is the same ~84-statement port, ending in the same unindexed
evidence_log COUNT (migrate.rs:247) that measured rows_read=3,156
against production D1. Flipping to the Rust port as-is makes the burn
strictly worse: every one of the ~540 requests/hour would pay the full
~3,157 rows, ~1.7M rows/hour, free tier gone by ~03:00Z. This patch is
therefore a PRE-FLIP requirement, not a follow-up.

THE FIX mirrors the worker.js patch exactly — same marker keys, same
SCHEMA_EPOCH string ("v0.7.3-a3-1") — because during the flip both
implementations share one D1 and must agree on the epoch:
1. New ensure_migrated() in migrate.rs: per-isolate AtomicBool fast
   path; otherwise ONE primary-key lookup
   (exchange_config.schema_epoch, 1 row); full migrate() only when the
   epoch is stale or unreadable; migrate() completion stamps the
   epoch. Errors propagate exactly as migrate() errors do today
   (handle() turns them into the 500 "exchange fault" response) and
   SCHEMA_OK is set only on success, so the next request retries —
   the JS __migrating reset semantics.
2. routes.rs: both call sites use ensure_migrated().
3. Inside migrate(): the backfill check reads the backfill_v03_done
   marker (PK, 1 row) instead of COUNTing evidence_log; the COUNT
   runs at most once more and writes the marker either way.
4. migrate() gains idx_evidence_log_trigger (same as the JS patch).

EXPECTED EFFECT: identical to the JS patch — cold-start cost per
isolate falls from ~84 read queries / ~3,157 rows to 2 / 2, warm
requests pay 0. Projected daily rows read ~0.3-0.5M at current
traffic, ~10x under the 5M free cap.

